---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
---

<Docs
  title="Provider swap"
  description="Migrate a project from one provider to an equivalent in the same category — auth, database, email, analytics, and more — in a single command."
  eyebrow="CLI"
>
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    <code>stack swap &lt;from&gt; &lt;to&gt;</code> replaces one provider with
    another in the same category by setting up the destination first, then
    attempting aliases and local cleanup of the source. It does not delete the
    old upstream resource or provide an atomic whole-swap guarantee. Inspect
    partial failures and remaining configuration or secrets.
  </p>

  <p>38 preset pairs are built in across six categories:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Category</th>
        <th>Supported pairs (bidirectional)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Auth</td>
        <td>clerk ↔ auth0, clerk ↔ workos, auth0 ↔ workos</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Database</td>
        <td>supabase ↔ neon, supabase ↔ turso, neon ↔ turso</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Email</td>
        <td>resend ↔ sendgrid, resend ↔ mailgun, resend ↔ postmark, sendgrid ↔ mailgun, sendgrid ↔ postmark, mailgun ↔ postmark</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Analytics</td>
        <td>posthog ↔ mixpanel, posthog ↔ plausible, mixpanel ↔ plausible</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Observability</td>
        <td>datadog ↔ grafana</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Deploy</td>
        <td>vercel ↔ netlify, vercel ↔ cloudflare, netlify ↔ cloudflare</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Basic usage</h2>

  <Code
    code={`# Migrate from Clerk to Auth0
stack swap clerk auth0

# Preview the plan without executing
stack swap clerk auth0 --dry-run`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>What happens under the hood:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Stack checks that <code>from</code> is configured in <code>.stack.toml</code>.</li>
    <li>Stack runs provider setup for <code>to</code>; authentication and resource operations depend on that adapter and its configured credentials.</li>
    <li>Unless <code>--keep-from</code> is set, Stack attempts Phantom secret cleanup and removes the local <code>from</code> service/MCP entry. The old upstream resource is kept; secrets can remain when terminal confirmation is unavailable or refused.</li>
    <li>Local configuration updates can be partial. A source-cleanup failure does not revert the destination setup; review warnings and finish cleanup manually.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Env-key aliases</h2>
  <p>
    Some pairs use different env variable names for the same concept. For
    example, Supabase writes <code>SUPABASE_URL</code> while Neon writes
    <code>DATABASE_URL</code>. After a <code>supabase → neon</code> swap, Stack
    attempts to mirror <code>DATABASE_URL</code> into
    <code>SUPABASE_URL</code> in Phantom. Stored-value access or a write can
    be refused, so an alias is not guaranteed; inspect warnings and configure
    the required value yourself through Phantom's trusted-terminal flow.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    Aliases are a compatibility shim, not a permanent solution. Update your code
    to reference the new provider's canonical key when you have a moment.
  </div>

  <h2>Flags</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 30%">Flag</th>
        <th>Behaviour</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--dry-run</code></td>
        <td>Print every step that would run without making any upstream API calls or writing any files.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--keep-from</code></td>
        <td>Provision <code>to</code> without removing <code>from</code>. Useful for a side-by-side migration where you want to validate the new provider before cutting over.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>--no-rollback</code></td>
        <td>Accepted by this source, but not used or forwarded by the swap implementation. It does not disable the destination add workflow's failure cleanup.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Rollback on failure</h2>
  <p>
    If destination setup fails after creating a resource, the add workflow
    attempts cleanup, including upstream teardown only when the provider
    supports it. Vault removal and teardown can fail or leave secrets stored;
    inspect the reported partial failure. Source removal begins only after
    destination setup succeeds, and later cleanup failure does not undo that
    success. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/add/#transactional-rollback">Transactional rollback</a> for
    the full failure-handling model.
  </p>

  <h2>Inspect available options</h2>

  <Code
    code={`# Inspect the options supported by your installed CLI
stack swap --help`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />
</Docs>
